  MCP server implementing the two delivery channels — handshake teaching surface and the install action — that carry AIDE doctrine from canonical docs into host projects. The install action has two entry points (the `aide_init` MCP tool and the npx CLI) into a single shared install service.
intent: >
  src/ is the MCP server that implements aidemd-mcp's two delivery channels.
  Channel one is the handshake itself: the tool list is read verbatim by
  every connecting client, and tool descriptions teach AIDE concepts at
  invocation time — heaviest in `aide_discover` and `aide_scaffold`, lighter
  in the operational tools. Channel two is the install action, exposed via
  the `aide_init` MCP tool and the npx CLI as two entry points into a single
  shared install service. The install service reads the canonical doc files
  under docs/ and installs them into a host project as a pointer stub in the
  host's config file, a progressively-disclosed methodology doc set on the
  host's disk, pipeline slash commands, MCP wiring, and IDE preview. Both
  channels pull from the same canonical docs — no AIDE doctrine is allowed
  to live inside this submodule's source code.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every lifecycle phase the AIDE methodology defines has a tool
      dedicated to it, and no two tools cover the same phase. The surface
      is verifiable as a one-to-one mapping between methodology phases and
      tool responsibilities — adding a new phase to the methodology is the
      only event that should require adding a tool.
    - Every tool description is complete enough to teach the convention it
      invokes on its own. An agent reading only the tool list learns what
      `.aide` files are, the file types that exist, the never-both-.aide-
      and-intent.aide naming rule, the next-to-orchestrators placement
      rule, and the read-specs-before-code workflow. No "see docs", no
      abbreviation, no assumed prior knowledge.
    - Tool descriptions, the pointer stub aide_init writes into the
      host's config file, and the methodology doc set aide_init
      installs onto the host's disk are all composed from the canonical
      doc files under docs/ at build or init time. The server's source
      code contains no AIDE doctrine as string literals, so a doc edit
      is the only way to change what this submodule ships.
    - Behavior is identical across every supported MCP client (Claude
      Code, Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot, Zed). Client-specific logic is
      confined to aide_init; every other tool is client-agnostic.
    - The install action turns a cold host project AIDE-ready in one
      invocation and is safely re-runnable, regardless of whether the
      entry point was the `aide_init` MCP tool or the npx CLI. Pointer
      stub written to the host's config file, methodology doc set
      installed onto the host's disk, slash commands scaffolded for
      every pipeline phase, MCP server wired into the project's MCP
      config, IDE file associations configured. The stub names the
      doc-set path the installer writes into, so the two sides cannot
      drift. Every step is idempotent.
  undesired:
    - A tool description that points at an external document or says the
      agent should "read CLAUDE.md for details." The handshake is the
      only documentation surface this channel is guaranteed to deliver.
    - A tool description, pointer-stub teaching sentence, or
      methodology doc hand-written as a string literal in this
      submodule's source, duplicating content that lives in docs/. The
      duplicate drifts on the first edit to either side and breaks the
      single-source-of-truth invariant inherited from the parent.
    - A pointer stub that names a host-side doc-set path different
      from the path the installer actually writes into. The two sides
      must derive from a single shared source inside this submodule;
      any arrangement that admits drift between them is broken on
      arrival even if it passes its unit tests.
    - Divergent behavior between MCP clients on the same input. If Claude
      Code and Cursor see different results from `aide_discover`, the
      server has failed its client-parity invariant.
    - An install run that is not idempotent — a project initialized
      twice, through either entry point, with stacked pointer stubs,
      duplicated doc-set files, duplicated slash commands, or a broken
      .mcp.json is a corrupted project, not an initialized one. The
      idempotency invariant lives at the install-service layer so both
      the `aide_init` MCP tool and the npx CLI inherit it identically.
    - Two tools whose responsibilities overlap on the same lifecycle
      phase, regardless of how the overlap was introduced — a new tool
      added because an existing one "didn't quite fit," or an existing
      tool quietly extended to cover a phase another tool already owns.
      Overlap fragments the handshake and forces agents to guess which
      tool to call, and is the only tool-surface change that should
      register as drift. Adding a tool for a phase no existing tool covers
      is not drift; it is the surface tracking the methodology.
    - A spec, test, or description that pins a specific count of tools or
      enumerates the tool roster as part of the contract. The count is an
      implementation detail of the current methodology snapshot, not an
      invariant — encoding it guarantees drift the moment a new lifecycle
      phase is added.
    - Truncated descriptions justified by "token budget." Tokens spent
      teaching are recovered every time an agent doesn't have to fetch
      an external doc or silently violate the convention.
---

## Context

This submodule implements both delivery channels the parent spec requires
and the runtime supporting plumbing the methodology depends on once the
host project is wired. The handshake channel matters because the tool
list is the only documentation surface a compliant MCP client is required
to present to the model verbatim, in every session, in every project,
without user action. The install action matters because a one-command
bootstrap is the only onboarding path that does not depend on the
installing agent remembering or paraphrasing what AIDE is — and that one
command is reachable two ways: the `aide_init` MCP tool when the user is
talking to an agent connected to this server, and the npx CLI when the
user has nothing connected yet. Both routes invoke the same install
service; neither route teaches AIDE doctrine. Between the handshake
(teaching) and the install action (artifact delivery), the parent's "no
paraphrasing, no per-project drift" guarantee lives or dies here.

Around those two channels sit the runtime and configuration surfaces that
make the methodology work after install. The runtime brain entry-point
tool reads the host's user-owned brain configuration file at
`.aide/config/brain.aide` and returns its hand-written agent-facing
prose section verbatim — the package no longer enumerates connectors,
infers brain identity from MCP entry shape, or composes per-connector
prose from inline templates. The npx CLI exposes a second verb,
`sync`, that propagates the user's `brain.aide` frontmatter into the
host's `.mcp.json` brain entry; it is the only path in the package that
mutates the host's MCP wiring, because every load-bearing contract
surface needs a visible command boundary the user typed. The MCP server
itself never silently rewrites `.mcp.json`. A boot-time probe surfaces
a four-state union (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`)
that the orchestrator consumes to decide whether the pipeline can
proceed or must halt and direct the user to recover. Drift between
`brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` is a hard halt — the package never
auto-repairs it.

Everything else — how many tools, how descriptions are written, how the
install action handles idempotency, how brain configuration is parsed,
how sync writes — is in service of keeping the teaching, install,
runtime, and configuration surfaces faithful to the canonical docs and
to the user's own configuration file.

## Strategy

**The tool list is the primary teaching artifact for channel one, not an
API reference.** Each description introduces the concept it invokes before
invoking it: `aide_discover` explains what `.aide` files are and why you
read them before code; `aide_scaffold` explains the naming-conflict rule
before it describes its `type` enum. An agent that only ever reads the
tool list must still come away with the full convention. The cost of a
longer description is recovered every time an agent doesn't need to fetch
an external doc, and dwarfed by the cost of an agent silently violating
the convention.

**The teaching weight across tool descriptions is uneven, and that is
deliberate.** `aide_discover` and `aide_scaffold` carry most of the
load — they introduce the file types, the placement and naming rules,
the read-before-code workflow. The remaining tool descriptions
(`aide_read`, `aide_validate`, `aide_upgrade`, `aide_info`,
`aide_inspect`, `aide_brain`, and the `aide_init` description itself)
are mostly operational, naming what the tool does without re-teaching
the methodology behind it. This is acceptable because the handshake is
not the only teaching surface. The install action installs a short
pointer stub into the host's framework config file (`CLAUDE.md` for
Claude Code, equivalent files for other clients) that the host agent
reads at session-start on every session, AIDE-related or not, and that
stub names the on-disk methodology doc set the agent crawls when a task
actually touches `.aide` material. Tool descriptions reinforce the
doctrine when an agent invokes a specific tool; the always-on
session-start teaching comes from the stub-plus-doc-set that channel
two installs. A tool description that is light on methodology is fine
when the channel-two installation is doing its job.

**Tool descriptions and host-installed methodology artifacts are composed
from `docs/`, never hand-written.** The canonical doc files under
`.aide/docs/` are read at build time (for tool description text) and at
install time (for both the pointer stub the install service writes into
the host's framework config file and the doc set it lands on the host's
disk). No AIDE doctrine lives as a string literal in this submodule's
source — doctrine lives in `docs/`, and this submodule is a renderer.
This is how the parent's single-source-of-truth invariant survives
contact with the server's runtime.

**Delivery to the host practices progressive disclosure at the file
layer, not just at the doc layer.** The install action does not dump the
full methodology into the host's framework config file. It writes a
short pointer stub into that file and lands the full methodology doc
set into a dedicated `.aide/docs/` directory on the host's disk. The
framework config file — which the agent reads on every session —
carries only the knowledge that AIDE exists, where the doc set lives,
and when to crawl into it. The doc set itself is read only by sessions
whose current task actually touches `.aide` files. The pattern mirrors
how the user's `study-playbook` skill walks the brain: a shallow
pointer surface that enumerates a deep content surface, with the agent
crawling on demand instead of carrying everything on every read.

**Each tool covers a distinct lifecycle phase with no overlap.** Discover
is the map; read is the drill; scaffold is the pen; validate is the
health check; init is the bootstrap; upgrade is the updater; brain is
the runtime entry point to the host's external knowledge store; info is
the boot probe. Adding a new tool requires showing that no existing tool
can absorb the responsibility without overlap. A tighter surface means
less for an agent to get wrong and less description budget competing
for attention at handshake time.

**Client parity via MCP, not per-client shims.** The server speaks
standard MCP over stdio. All client-specific branching lives in the
install service, which writes framework-specific config files for
whichever framework it detects. The other tools are fed identical input
schemas regardless of which client is talking, because parity is what
lets the teaching surface be reused across clients.

**Two install entry points, one install service.** The `aide_init` MCP
tool and the npx CLI are entry points into the same install service
that lives under the install module. They diverge only in their wire
shape — the MCP tool exchanges structured JSON with a calling agent
and pauses for inherent decisions through `AskUserQuestion`; the CLI
runs end-to-end in a terminal session and pauses for inherent
decisions through stdin prompts (or accepts them up-front via narrowly
scoped escape-hatch flags whose only purpose is to supply, at
invocation time, the answer to an inherent decision the CLI would
otherwise defer — the brain root path and the IDE choice are the two
deferral surfaces today, each with one corresponding flag). The
install service itself is consumer-agnostic: same plan shape, same
status enum, same idempotency contract, same prescriptions for MCP
config merge. Adding a new entry point is permissible if and only if
the new entry point installs (it does not teach AIDE doctrine); a CLI
that walks an agent through the methodology interactively is the
forbidden third channel, not a thin invocation of channel two.

**Idempotency as an invariant, not a feature.** The install action,
scaffold, validate, sync, and upgrade are all safe to re-run on a
project already in that state. Agents retry tool calls when uncertain;
CLI users re-run commands when uncertain; a non-idempotent call
corrupts on the retry. This is especially load-bearing for the install
action and for sync, both of which mutate files neither an agent nor a
developer should feel nervous about re-running.

**Brain configuration is user-owned, not code-owned, and the package
never branches on connector identity.** The host's brain is described
by a single user-editable file at `.aide/config/brain.aide`. Its
frontmatter carries exactly two fields — `name` (a human-readable
label) and `mcpServerConfig` (`command` and `args`, consumed by sync
to write the host's `.mcp.json` brain entry). Its body is three
marker-bounded sections in fixed order — prose, playbook, research —
each delimited by its own paired HTML-comment open/close tokens,
recognized lowercase and case-sensitive. No code in this submodule
enumerates connectors, dispatches on a `name` value, or composes
per-connector prose from inline templates. The runtime brain
entry-point tool reads the parsed `prose` section and returns it
verbatim; if a future user wires a connector the package has never
heard of, that connector's hand-written prose ships through unchanged
because there is nothing in the response path that needs to recognize
it. Adding, replacing, or retargeting a brain is a host-side edit to
one file followed by `npx aidemd-mcp sync` — never a code change to
this package.

**Per-consumer ownership of `brain.aide` is exclusive.** A single
canonical parser owns the file format and exposes a typed result;
every other surface in this submodule consumes only the slice it is
contracted to read. The runtime brain entry-point tool reads `prose`
only — never `playbook`, never `research`, never frontmatter for
control flow. The install service's brain provisioner reads `playbook`
and `research` to seed the brain's playbook and research entry-point
artifacts at scaffold time — never `prose`. The sync verb and the boot
probe read frontmatter only — none of the body sections. Cross-section
reads are contract violations because they couple a consumer to bytes
it has no authority over and leak the file format outward. The
single-parser-many-consumers shape is the same chokepoint pattern the
canonical doc reader already uses — one reader, many composers — and
is the load-bearing decision behind the marker-bounded body grammar:
named sections make ownership visible in the type signatures every
consumer imports.

**Sync is the only `.mcp.json` mutation surface for the brain entry,
and the boundary is a typed terminal command.** The npx CLI exposes a
`sync` verb that reads `.aide/config/brain.aide`, takes
`mcpServerConfig.command` and the post-interpolation args as the
expected entry, and writes them into the host's `.mcp.json` under the
fixed `brain` key — preserving every other key in `mcpServers`
verbatim. Sync is idempotent. The MCP server NEVER silently rewrites
`.mcp.json`, the runtime brain entry-point tool never patches the
host's MCP wiring, and the boot probe never auto-repairs drift it
detects. Every mutation of the host's MCP brain wiring is a command
the user typed with output the user saw scroll past. The reason is
trust: `.mcp.json` is a load-bearing config the user often hand-edits
to point a brain at a temporary debug location while reproducing a
bug, and any mutation from inside an agent session would silently
overwrite that hand-edit. The visible-command-boundary invariant is
the protection the user relies on.

**Brain status is a four-state tagged union and the orchestrator halts
on every non-`ok` state.** The boot probe surfaces exactly four states:
`ok` (`.aide/config/brain.aide` parses, `.mcp.json` has a brain entry,
the two agree), `no-brain-aide` (the source-of-truth file is missing,
malformed-frontmatter, or malformed-body), `no-mcp-entry`
(`brain.aide` parses but `.mcp.json` has no brain entry under
`mcpServers`), and `mcp-drift` (both exist but disagree by structural
comparison on `command` or `args`). Each state names exactly one user
recovery action — run `npx aidemd-mcp init`, run `npx aidemd-mcp sync`,
run `npx aidemd-mcp sync` — and the orchestrator refuses to advance
the pipeline until the user takes it. There is no `invalid-path`
state: the package no longer stats the brain root path, because the
schema collapse removed `rootPath` and path validation is the
launcher's job at MCP server startup. Reintroducing a fifth state
under any name shifts a load-bearing contract surface back into the
package.

**Substitution is partitioned into one surface, run only at install
and sync time, never at agent time.** The parser supports `${...}`
interpolation of frontmatter field values inside
`mcpServerConfig.args` for advanced users who want to DRY a value
across argument positions; the default scaffold makes no use of it
(the brain root path lives byte-inline in `args`). The interpolation
surface runs only when the install service is deriving the brain MCP
entry's args at scaffold time, or when sync is computing the entry
to write into `.mcp.json`. It NEVER runs against any of the three
body sections — `prose`, `playbook`, and `research` all return
verbatim, byte-identical to what the user wrote between their marker
pairs. A user whose prose legitimately documents a templating
language whose syntax includes `${...}` characters reads exactly
those characters out of `aide_brain`'s response. Mixing the install-
time substitution surface with the agent-time body surfaces is the
exact failure mode the parent spec's bad examples enumerate, and
keeping the surfaces in separate exported helpers makes the mistake
impossible by accident.

**The `.aide/config/` directory is user-owned forever after the first
scaffold.** The install service may create `.aide/config/` and seed
the canonical default `brain.aide` on a cold install where the file
is absent. After that first write, every path under `.aide/config/`
belongs to the user — the install service and the upgrade tool never
overwrite, patch, or migrate anything in that directory on subsequent
runs, regardless of byte drift from the canonical default. The
boundary is structural (the directory path itself is the ownership
signal), not a per-file allowlist, so any future user-owned config
file added under `.aide/config/` inherits the same semantics
automatically. New defaults reach existing hosts only through `npx
aidemd-mcp sync` (for the derived `.mcp.json` brain entry) or a
deliberate user edit (for the file itself), never through the install
or upgrade path. The same class of failure that a silent MCP server
rewriting `.mcp.json` would produce — a load-bearing user surface
shifting out from under the user — applies to anything inside
`.aide/config/`.

**The CLI is the sole cold-start surface and runs unattended; the MCP
tool is the agent-mediated surface and prompts inherently.** The npx
CLI installs every canonical artifact whose content carries no
inherent decision attached and skips on conflict, because it has no
agent to interview the user mid-run; categories that depend on
inherent decisions (the brain root path, which scaffolds
`.aide/config/brain.aide` and the derived MCP entry; the IDE choice)
are deferred to the right follow-up surface unless supplied as
flags. The `aide_init` MCP tool runs the same install service through
an agent that can ask the user via `AskUserQuestion`, and prompts on
overwrite-pending categories and on inherent decisions. Both produce
the same on-disk result on a fully-resolved run — the consumer is the
wire shape, the install service is the contract.

## Good examples

A new agent connects to aidemd-mcp for the first time and, before
touching a single file, reasons:

> There are .aide spec files next to orchestrator code that I should
> read before I read the code. The canonical file types are: `.aide`,
> the default intent spec; `intent.aide`, the same thing renamed when
> a `research.aide` sits alongside; `research.aide`, raw research;
> `plan.aide`, the architect's checkboxed implementation plan;
> `todo.aide`, a QA re-alignment document; `brain.aide`, the host's
> user-owned brain configuration file. I must never see both `.aide`
> and `intent.aide` in the same folder. My workflow is: call
> `aide_discover` with no path for the project map, then drill into
> the relevant subtree with a path, then `aide_read` specific specs as
> needed.

Every piece of that reasoning came from the tool list alone. No
CLAUDE.md was read; no README was fetched. That is the target experience
at handshake time.

A user invokes the install action on a fresh project — either by asking
the agent to call `aide_init`, or by running `npx aidemd-mcp init` in a
terminal — and gets back a summary like:

> Detected framework: Claude Code.
> Created CLAUDE.md with AIDE pointer stub naming the methodology doc set at .aide/docs/.
> Installed .aide/docs/ with the canonical methodology docs and an index.
> Created slash commands for the pipeline phases.
> Scaffolded .aide/config/brain.aide (the user-owned brain config) at the supplied brain root path.
> Wired aidemd-mcp into .mcp.json under the `aide` key, and the brain entry derived from .aide/config/brain.aide under the `brain` key.
> Configured .zed/settings.json to render .aide as Markdown.
> Skipped VS Code extension — `code` CLI not on PATH.

Running the same install action a second time, through either entry
point, returns the same summary with "already present" markers instead
of "created", and nothing on disk changes — including
`.aide/config/brain.aide`, which the install service never re-touches
after its first scaffold even if the user has retargeted the brain
root, swapped the launcher command, or rewritten the prose section.
The install service is what guarantees this; the entry point is just a
wire shape.

A maintainer edits `docs/aide-spec.md` to clarify the placement rule.
They rebuild the server; the next agent to connect sees the updated
text in the `aide_discover` tool description because the description
template reads from the doc at build time. They also run the install
action on a host project; the updated text lands in the host's
`.aide/docs/aide-spec.md` because the methodology installer reads the
same canonical source. One edit propagated through both channels —
handshake teaching and installed host-side content — with no
duplication in between.

An agent in a host project opens a file unrelated to AIDE to fix a bug.
It reads CLAUDE.md at session start, sees the short pointer stub,
registers that the `.aide/docs/` directory exists, and consciously
skips crawling it because the current task touches no `.aide` files. Total AIDE cost
paid by that session: a handful of lines of stub. Later the same agent
picks up a task that does touch `.aide` files, follows the stub's
pointer into `.aide/docs/`, reads the index, and drills into
`aide-spec.md` and `aide-template.md` — and only those. The delivery
layer practiced the same progressive disclosure the methodology
teaches.

A user retargets their brain to a new location. They open
`.aide/config/brain.aide` and edit a single string inside
`mcpServerConfig.args` (the path next to `"@bitbonsai/mcpvault"`),
then run `npx aidemd-mcp sync` in their terminal:

> Read .aide/config/brain.aide
> Wrote brain MCP entry into .mcp.json
>   command: npx
>   args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/brains/new-location"]
> Done.

Every other key under `mcpServers` in `.mcp.json` is byte-identical to
what was there before — the user's `aide` entry, their personal MCP
servers, every entry the package did not author. The user runs sync a
second time immediately to test idempotency: sync reads the same file,
computes the same target object, sees `.mcp.json` already matches, and
exits with "no changes." The host's MCP wiring was mutated exactly
once, on a command the user typed, with the user looking at the
terminal. The MCP server itself never silently rewrote anything.

A pipeline agent mid-task calls `aide_brain` to read its brain
instructions. The host's `.aide/config/brain.aide` body opens with:

```
<!-- aide-prose-start -->
Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.
Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-root-relative
path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across the
brain. The brain's entry-point file is `CLAUDE.md` at the brain root
— read that first; it lists the inline references your task may need.
When a reference in the entry-point file looks relevant to your task,
read the file it names (depth 1) and check the references inside that
file too. Stay in scope; don't follow references into unrelated topics.
<!-- aide-prose-end -->
```

`aide_brain` returns:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.\nUse `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files..."
}
```

The bytes between the prose marker pair are returned byte-identical:
no template substitution, no server-composed wrapping, no `name` field
on the response, no `connector` discriminant for the consumer to
branch on. The agent reads the user's instructions verbatim and
follows them. If the same user later switches brains by editing
`.aide/config/brain.aide` to declare a different connector and
rewriting the prose section to describe it, the next call to
`aide_brain` returns the new prose with no code change in this
package — because there is no code in the response path that needed
to know what brand of brain the user wired.

A boot probe runs at the start of an agent session and finds drift —
the user retargeted `brain.aide` and forgot to run sync. The probe
returns `mcp-drift`. The orchestrator surfaces a remediation message
naming the exact terminal command — `npx aidemd-mcp sync` — and
refuses to advance the pipeline. The package never auto-rewrites
`.mcp.json` to "fix" the drift, because the user may have
intentionally hand-edited the brain entry in `.mcp.json` for a debug
session and an auto-repair would silently destroy that hand-edit. The
user runs sync, restarts Claude Code, and the next boot returns `ok`.

## Bad examples

A tool description that reads "Scaffold new .aide files. See the AIDE
docs for naming rules." The agent has learned nothing about the
naming-conflict rule and will happily create a `.aide` next to an
existing `research.aide`, producing exactly the corruption the tool was
built to prevent. The description failed its only job.

A tool description hand-written as a template literal in
`src/tools/scaffold/index.ts`, containing AIDE rules the maintainer
typed from memory. The canonical doc under `docs/` has since been
updated; the literal has not; the two now disagree, and the server's
handshake ships a different doctrine than the install action does. The
single-source-of-truth invariant has been broken at the submodule
level.

An install action that appends its pointer stub to CLAUDE.md on every
run, so a project initialized three times — through any combination of
entry points — has three stacked marker-bounded regions. Idempotency
was treated as a nice-to-have instead of an invariant living at the
service layer, and the file is now corrupted.

An install action that skips the methodology-doc-set step and dumps
the full methodology body inside CLAUDE.md's stub region instead.
Every non-AIDE session in the host now re-reads several hundred lines
of doctrine on every open, and the progressive-disclosure rule that
this delivery shape was meant to enforce has been quietly reversed at
the delivery layer while passing every test.

A tool — say `aide_lint` — added to the surface because validation
rules "felt different" from health checks. Both tools show up in the
handshake, overlap in responsibility, and the agent has to guess which
one to call. The surface has been diluted for no gain, because the
responsibility should have lived inside validate.

The runtime brain entry-point tool dispatches the host's `.mcp.json`
brain entry through a package-side registry of "known launchers,"
identifies the connector by matching argument shapes against a table,
and selects per-connector prose composed from inline TypeScript
constants. A user wires a connector the registry has never heard of
and gets back "no brain configured" even though their `brain.aide`
exists and parses cleanly — the package literally cannot see brains
its registry was not taught to recognize. The retired in-code backend
registry has been re-introduced under a new name. The fix is to read
`.aide/config/brain.aide`'s parsed prose section and return it
verbatim; what the user wrote is what the agent reads, regardless of
what brand of brain it describes.

The MCP server detects drift between `.aide/config/brain.aide` and
`.mcp.json` at startup and "helpfully" auto-syncs by overwriting
`.mcp.json`'s brain entry from the source-of-truth file. The user
wakes up the next morning and finds their `.mcp.json` mutated by
something that was not a command they typed. Worse: the prior
afternoon they had hand-edited `.mcp.json` to point the brain MCP
server at a temporary debug location while reproducing a bug, and
that hand-edit is silently gone. The visible-command-boundary
invariant has been violated. Drift is a hard halt, not a self-repair
event; the only thing the boot probe is allowed to do with drift is
name it and route the user to `npx aidemd-mcp sync`.

The brain entry-point tool returns a `name` field alongside `status`
and `instructions` to "let the calling agent identify the connected
brain." Within a release, two consumers branch on the value of `name`
to vary their behavior — one only enables a research-write shortcut
when `name` matches a particular hard-coded label, another keys a
special-case prompt off another label. The connector-dispatch
anti-pattern the registry retirement was meant to delete has been
re-created on the consumer side. The response shape is `status` +
`instructions` only; the user's `name` label belongs inside
`brain.aide` for the human reading the file, never threaded into the
agent-facing wire.

The runtime brain entry-point tool runs `${...}` substitution over the
prose section before returning it — the same install-time
interpolation surface that lives in `mcpServerConfig.args`, applied
to the agent-facing body section. A user whose prose legitimately
documents a templating language whose syntax includes literal
`${...}` characters has those characters silently rewritten or
stripped, mangling the agent-facing instructions in a way invisible
from the package's tests. Substitution lives only in
`mcpServerConfig.args` at install/sync time. None of the three body
sections — prose, playbook, research — receives any agent-time or
disk-time substitution.

The install service overwrites `.aide/config/brain.aide` on a
subsequent install run because "the canonical default has improved
since the user's last install." The user retargeted their brain to a
custom path two months ago, refined the prose section to suit their
own workflow, and renamed their brain. All three edits are silently
gone. `.aide/config/` is user-owned forever after the first scaffold;
the install and upgrade tools must never overwrite, patch, or migrate
anything inside that directory, regardless of byte drift from the
canonical default. New defaults reach existing hosts through `npx
aidemd-mcp sync` (for the derived `.mcp.json` brain entry) or a
deliberate user edit (for the file itself), never through the install
or upgrade path.

A `brain.aide` carrying deprecated frontmatter fields — `connector`,
`rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools` — is silently accepted by the parser
and the offending fields are stripped before downstream consumers see
the parsed result. The user's `tools.read` declaration is sitting
right there in their file, but nothing in the package consumes it;
months later they wonder why changing it has no effect. Silent
acceptance is the worst possible failure mode for a closed schema:
rejection forces clean migration, while silent stripping creates
dead-field drift the user never finds out about. The parser must
reject deprecated fields by name with `malformed-frontmatter`
naming every stale field it found.

A brain.aide carried forward from the pre-pivot heading-based body
schema — body sections were marked by Markdown headings, not by paired
HTML-comment markers — is silently auto-migrated by the parser: it
detects the old shape, infers the section boundaries from the
headings, and returns the parsed result as if the new marker grammar
were satisfied. The user never edits their file; the heading-based
shape persists in the wild forever; the install service's scaffold
disagrees with what the parser will accept; deprecation never lands
because there is no forcing event. Strict failure is the only shape
that produces a forcing event: pre-pivot files return `malformed-body`
naming every missing marker, the user hand-edits their file once, the
heading-based shape disappears from the wild on the next sync.

The boot probe collapses the four-state union down to two states —
`ok` and `not-ok` — "to keep the orchestrator's branching simple."
Now the orchestrator's remediation prose cannot tell the user what to
do because every `not-ok` shape has a different recovery path
(scaffold the file, run sync to write the entry, run sync to align
the entry). Vocabulary that lies about the recovery surface is worse
than vocabulary that admits which recovery is needed. The four states
are load-bearing precisely because each names a distinct user action.

A second `.mcp.json` mutation surface is added — say, a tool that
"helpfully" patches the host's MCP wiring inside a session "to save
the user from running sync." Now there are two paths that mutate
`.mcp.json`: the one the user typed and saw, and the one the agent
ran silently. Within a few releases users no longer trust that the
file they last edited still says what they wrote, the
visible-command-boundary protection is gone, and the boot probe's
`mcp-drift` state stops being meaningful because `.mcp.json` may have
been rewritten by something other than sync. The contract is exactly
one mutation surface per host file, with the user typing the command.

